WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:03.033
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.033 --> 00:00:06.819
It is time for a position paper.

00:00:06.819 --> 00:00:12.160
This paper does not have the usual visual
fireworks that you see in many of these videos,

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:17.909
however, it addresses the cornerstone of scientific
publication, which is none other than peer

00:00:17.909 --> 00:00:19.009
review.

00:00:19.009 --> 00:00:23.130
When a research group is done with a project,
they don’t just write up the results and

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:29.040
chuck the paper into a repository, but instead,
they submit it to a scientific venue, for

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.160
instance, a journal or a conference.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.420
Then, the venue finds several other researchers
who are willing to go through the work with

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:38.629
a fine-tooth comb.

00:00:38.629 --> 00:00:44.780
In the case of double-blind reviews, both
the authors and the reviewers remain anonymous

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:45.809
to each other.

00:00:45.809 --> 00:00:52.059
The reviewers now check whether the results
are indeed significant, novel, credible and

00:00:52.059 --> 00:00:53.589
reproducible.

00:00:53.589 --> 00:00:59.239
If the venue is really good, this process
is very tough and thorough, and this is process

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:04.680
becomes the scientific version of beating
the heck out of someone, but in a constructive

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:05.680
manner.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:11.770
If the work is able to withstand serious criticism,
and ticks the required boxes, it can proceed

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:13.920
to get published at this venue.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.090
Otherwise, it is rejected.

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:21.299
So what we heard so far is that the research
work is being reviewed, however, scientists

00:01:21.299 --> 00:01:27.300
at the Google AI lab raised the issue that
the reviewers themselves should also be reviewed.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:31.790
Consider the fact that all scientists are
expected to spend a certain percentage of

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.590
their time to serve the greater good.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:39.900
For instance, throughout my PhD studies, I
have reviewed over 30 papers and I am not

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.220
even done yet.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.939
These paper reviews take place without compensation.

00:01:43.939 --> 00:01:47.299
Let’s call this issue number one for now.

00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:52.149
Issue number two is the explosive growth of
the number of submissions over time at the

00:01:52.149 --> 00:01:56.360
most prestigious machine learning and computer
vision conferences.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:57.360
Have a look here.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.499
It is of utmost importance that we create
a review system that is as fair as possible

00:02:02.499 --> 00:02:07.649
- after all, thousands of hours spent on research
projects are at stake.

00:02:07.649 --> 00:02:12.190
Add these two issues together, and we get
a system where the average quality of the

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:15.980
reviews will almost certainly decrease over
time.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:16.980
Quoting the authors:

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.690
“We believe the key issues here are structural.

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:25.830
Reviewers donate their valuable time and expertise
anonymously as a service to the community

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:32.890
with no compensation or attribution, are increasingly
taxed by a rapidly increasing number of submissions,

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:35.620
and are held to no enforced standards.”

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:42.110
In Two Minute Papers episode number 84, so
more than 200 episodes ago, we discussed the

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:43.650
NeurIPS experiment.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:47.810
Leave a comment if you’ve been around back
then and you enjoyed Two Minute Papers before

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:48.810
it was cool!

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:54.210
But don’t worry if this is not the case,
this was long ago, so here is a short summary:

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:59.780
a large amount of papers were secretly disseminated
to multiple committees, who would review it

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:04.930
without knowing about each other, and we would
have a look whether they would accept or reject

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:06.650
the same papers.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:10.810
Re-review papers and see if the results are
the same, if you will.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:16.200
If we use sophisticated mathematics to create
new scientific methods, why not use mathematics

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.610
to evaluate our own processes?

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:24.721
So, after doing that, it was found that at
a given prescribed acceptance ratio, there

00:03:24.721 --> 00:03:29.050
was a disagreement for 57% of the papers.

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.880
So, is this number good or bad?

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:37.720
Let’s imagine a completely hypothetical
committee that has no idea what they are doing,

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:43.270
and as a review, they basically toss up a
coin and accept or reject the paper based

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:45.080
on the result of the cointoss.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:47.810
Let’s call them the Coinflip Committee.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:52.200
The calculations conclude that the Coinflip
Committee would have a disagreement ratio

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.890
of about 77%.

00:03:53.890 --> 00:04:02.130
So, experts, 57% disagreement, Coinflip Committee,
77% disagreement.

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:07.250
And now, to answer whether this is good or
bad: this is hardly something to be proud

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:13.230
of — the consistency of expert reviewers
is significantly closer to a coinflip than

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:16.500
to a hypothetical perfect review process.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.560
If that is not an indication that we have
to do something about this, I am not sure

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:21.560
what is.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:26.680
So, in this paper, the authors propose two
important changes to the system to remedy

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:28.449
these issues:

00:04:28.449 --> 00:04:33.759
Remedy number one - they propose a rubric,
a 7-point document to evaluate the quality

00:04:33.759 --> 00:04:34.900
of the reviews.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:39.370
Again, not only the papers are reviewed, but
the reviews themselves.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:44.720
It is similar to the ones used in public schools
to evaluate student performance to make sure

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:50.120
whether the review was objective, consistent
and fair.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:55.350
Remedy number two - reviewers should be incentivized
and rewarded for their work.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:05:00.900
The authors argue that a professional service
should be worthy of professional compensation.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:05.740
Now, of course, this sounds great, but this
also requires money.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.639
Where should the funds come from?

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:13.100
The paper discusses several options: for instance,
this could be funded through sponsorships,

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:19.040
or, asking for a reasonable fee when submitting
a paper for peer review, and introducing a

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.260
new fee structure for science conferences.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:27.919
This is a short, 5-page paper that is very
easily readable for everyone, raises excellent

00:05:27.919 --> 00:05:33.419
points for a very important problem, so needless
to say, I highly recommend that you give it

00:05:33.419 --> 00:05:36.680
a read, as always, the link is in the video
description.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:40.370
I hope this video will help raising more awareness
to this problem.

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:45.629
If we are to create a fair system for evaluating
research papers, we better get this right.

00:05:45.629 --> 00:05:49.449
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

